ACCESS(2)                      P32W Unix                       ACCESS(2)

NAME
       access - determine the accessibility of a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int access(const char *path, int mode);

DESCRIPTION
       access checks whether the calling process may use the file
       path as mode says, using its real user and group IDs rather
       than the effective ones; a set-user-ID program can so ask
       what its user could do. mode is F_OK (0), to ask only
       whether the file exists, or an or of

       R_OK (4)   read
       W_OK (2)   write
       X_OK (1)   execute (search, for a directory)

       The directories on the way are searched with the real IDs
       too. For the super-user every check passes, except X_OK on a
       regular file, which needs at least one x bit.

RETURN VALUE
       0 if every access asked for is allowed; -1 otherwise, with
       errno set.

ERRORS
       EACCES  An access in mode is denied, or search permission
               is denied on a directory in the path.
       ENOENT  path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EFAULT  path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       stat(2), chmod(2), setuid(2), test(1)
